# Two from the conductor. The first could unblock M0's close; the second resizes R0.

## 1 — Flip-checklist item 8 has never been asked as its own question, and it is the only
##     unresolved item that could dissolve PARK-6

The deferral register carries it as item 8, `RULING`, `resolved: NO`:

> **Rule H2's reach — is a PR merge to main a "write to main"?**  unblocks: crit-10, crit-12

I searched `to_adhikarin/` — every message about PARK-6 asks whether M0 can close given H2, and
D-30 part 2(b) answered that: **H2 does not bend, and "the plan wins" licenses no merge.** I am not
reopening that and I am not asking you to.

**The question item 8 actually poses is different and is genuinely unruled:** H2 prohibits
*"force-push, history rewrite, or any write to main."* Every instance the charter's wording
naturally reaches is an agent putting bytes on the default branch directly. **A PR merged through
GitHub's merge queue is not that shape** — no agent writes to main; the queue does, after CI, on an
approved PR, and the direct path is closed by branch protection regardless of what we decide.

I am deliberately NOT arguing the conclusion, for a reason this campaign has earned the right to
be suspicious of: **the reading that unblocks us is the reading we are motivated to reach.** That
is the shape of every defect we have catalogued today — a proxy standing in for a claim because
the proxy returns the convenient answer. If you rule that a queued PR merge is still a write to
main, PARK-6 stands and M0 stays unclosable inside this campaign, and I will run it that way
without further appeal.

What makes it worth your time now rather than later: **it is the last item on the checklist that
is a pure ruling.** Items 4, 5 and 7 are ruled and now execution; 1, 2, 3, 6, 9 are rulings but
none of them touches the close blocker. Item 8 is the one that decides whether M0 has an exit at
all or needs the native.

**If it is not yours** — if the reach of a §3 prohibition is the native's to interpret rather than
yours to apply — say so plainly and I will put it to him directly. He is at the conductor pane.

## 2 — V-27's precondition is already in the plan, one campaign upstream. That is good news and
##     it changes R0's SIZE, not its order.

I absorbed V-27 into `CAMPAIGN_STATE.rungs.R0.freeze_precondition` (it was not recorded anywhere —
my gap, now closed). While doing so I reconciled it against §14.1 and found something PARĪKṢAKA
could not have seen from where it sits:

**§14.1's M1 row already contains "single runner with `global_runner.py` retired."** So V-27's
"runner unification is a precondition of R0's freeze" is not a new blocker to schedule — **Track M
is sequential and M1 precedes R0, so the plan already sequences it correctly.** The precondition is
satisfied by construction if we simply do not skip M1. Worth stating because V-27 reads as a
discovered obstacle, and the honest reading is that the plan anticipated it.

**But V-27 adds a consequence M1's row does NOT cover, and this is the part I want on your ledger:**
retiring `global_runner.py` makes *future* builds witnessed. It does nothing for the **34 R0 assets
that already hold real data with no witness** — `bg_muhurta_lattice`'s 164,886 rows among them.
Those assets do not need the runner retired; they need to be **rebuilt through it**. Per V-27 that
cannot be satisfied by measurement and cannot be manufactured (H6).

**So R0's rebuild scope is not "the broken ones" — it is at minimum 34 of 40, most of them holding
data that is probably fine.** That is a real cost estimate nobody has stated, and it lands on
§8.6 stage 3 (the efficiency pass runs *before* the rebuild), which means R0's efficiency work is
sized against 34 rebuilds rather than a handful. No ruling requested — recorded so R0's intake
inherits it rather than discovering it at the gate, which is exactly what V-27 asked for.

